 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel is processed once, decomposed into 6 tetrahedra.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + E)$ where $V$ is the number of output vertices and $E$ is the number of edges. The `edge_cache` requires memory proportional to the surface area.
-*   **Performance:** Binary MT is significantly faster than CA Smoothing MT (~13-15 seconds vs ~161.5 seconds on a 3.5M mm³ MRI T1 dataset) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Comparison to Marching Cubes:** This method is slower than standard Marching Cubes and creates more vertices, but it better represents the mesh surface by avoiding topological ambiguities [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Topologically correct, watertight meshes without ambiguous cases.
-*   **Limitations:** Slower than Marching Cubes; higher vertex count.
+* **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel is processed once, decomposed into 6 tetrahedra [S1, unofficial].
+* **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + E)$ where $V$ is the number of output vertices and $E$ is the number of edges. The `edge_cache` requires memory proportional to the surface area [G].
+* **Performance:** Binary MT is significantly faster than CA Smoothing MT (~13-15 seconds vs ~161.5 seconds on a 3.5M mm³ MRI T1 dataset) [S2, unofficial]. The First Algorithm spends 11 seconds to cross all data volume compared to 48 seconds for the Second Algorithm [S1, unofficial].
+* **Comparison to Marching Cubes:** This method is slower than standard Marching Cubes and creates more vertices, but it better represents the mesh surface by avoiding ambiguous cases [S3, unofficial].
+* **Strengths:** Topologically correct, watertight meshes without ambiguous cases. Generates fewer triangles than the Second Algorithm (ratio between 1.5 and 3), resulting in lower memory requirements and faster execution time [S1, unofficial].
+* **Limitations:** Slower than Marching Cubes; higher vertex count; potentially higher radius/area/volume error compared to the alternative decomposition [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced as a stable alternative to Marching Cubes to resolve topological ambiguities. It was implemented in the Invesalius project (Rust backend) and is also present in C# implementations like the one by Jason Fisher [S1, S2, unofficial]. The approach relies on the mathematical property that a tetrahedron cannot have the same topological ambiguity as a cube for a linear scalar field.
